e is  actually more of a driving force than  than other signals  uh and with that i'd like to thank uh my  lab specifically jifang zhao who's  really done the vast majority of this  work  and then gargi tony nifon  and members of jeff kidd's lab jeff and  sarah who are helping us with the  validation um i i put a link up here to  the bit bucket of the current code  please keep in mind this is an alpha so  we're we're still working on the readme  we're still getting it streamlined for  for better use i was hoping to have it  better by this step but you know that's  research for you  and also the funding agencies so um  thank you and i guess i will take  questions now  um  and um  let's see here okay so i'm supposed to  read these here so  what is the minimum read length that  your method needs  does it work on exome data are all the  exon ends problematic for example  reducing read depth um currently this is  based off of whole genome i in theory it  could work on exome data but it's not  designed that way and would probably  need to be  retooled a little bit to do that um  the uh the minimum read length  it's not really a minimum read length  but typically you know we've been doing  most of our work with hundred base pairs  so i i would say at least you know the  standard you know aluminum two by  hundreds would would be fine longer  would be better um 75 base pairs would  probably work fine but i think anything  below that you're going to start to lose  some of your uh segmentation in the  genome uh the signal from that  do you allow multizygosity so yes there  is a feature to increase the initial  number of structures from two to three  we haven't really tested this at any  large scale but we have included that  feature um to you know for for you know  non-diploid species  but again it there's no reason it  shouldn't work it will increase the run  time though because now you're  permutating three structures and not  just two  why can't these regions be investigated  using traditional sanger sequencing well  they can and in fact they many cases  they should i think the question is how  would you know to investigate that  region for example if you have what's  called a simple deletion you know you  may not be interested in going in there  and investigating that region but if you  know it's complex now all of a sudden it  might be a candidate for your targeted  sequencing approach so i i think i mean  i'm a big fan of validation i'm a big  fan of experimental experimentally going  in there proving what what the computers  are saying  um  but uh but you kind of need to know  where to start to do that and a lot of  times uh if you don't know there's a  complex event there if you don't know  there's inserted sequence in your  deletion your senior may not work  because you're you know maybe it's too  large and you expect a deletion but  there's actually you know a large  insertion there so  um  that's part of the challenges with that  and we hope that this  algorithm will will help guide some of  that  molecular work  and i think a final question do you have  a sense of what proportion of previously  annotated  uh simple insertions or simple deletions  may in fact represent more complex  rearrangements that's a very good  question and that's the question we're  trying to ask um i would be comfortable  saying that it's probably less than five  percent  um of what's been found before but i i  can't put a firm value on that um  because we're still going in and  actually figuring out what might be  spurry's predictions of our algorithm  we're still trying to um figure out the  best thresholds and like i said we're  doing um validation right now um  but um but i i would probably say less  than five percent i have seen observed  some really interesting ones though  where you have you know duplications of  exons that are inverted and inserted  nearby what that what does that do to  splicing what does that do to  isoform regulation i have 